From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 16:57:08 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 11091/11362] Resolve the silent child: a one-minute log lag,
 not a death

Last hour a conductor child had run 1h15m, was gone from /proc, and had written
no verdict, with the log's newest line over an hour old. I declined to write it
up because it was five minutes old and might resolve.

It resolved. The verdict landed at 20:24Z -- ARC live producer evidence contract,
hard wall-clock cap after 4802s. I had checked at 20:23:37Z. So the child left
/proc about a minute before its verdict reached the log, and a reader inside that
window sees a process that vanished without trace, which is precisely the shape of
a real silent death.

Holding the claim for an hour cost nothing and produced a clean answer. Writing it
up would have put a fictitious incident in the permanent record, which is the
failure the name-no-killer-you-cannot-evidence rule exists to prevent.

Enforced where it is needed rather than only recorded: the hourly status-check
prompt now carries the lag note, so the next reader is warned before they can
misread the window.

Also notes that the capped task was an ARC one, which softens without overturning
the 10-of-67 ARC-coverage figure in the progress assessment.

